SSH in
    ssh bandit6@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220#

  The password for the next level is stored somewhere on the server and has all of the following properties:
  
  owned by user bandit7
  owned by group bandit6
  33 bytes in size

We use the find command for this level, but we must run it from the root directory as the file can be anywhere in the server.

    bandit6@bandit:~$ cd /
    bandit6@bandit:/$ 
We utilise the options that are available for the find command. These can be seen from the manual

bandit6@bandit:/$ man find

Looking at the manual, we try and see what options would help us find the file using the given information.
    -user uname
            File is owned by user uname (numeric user ID allowed).
    -group gname
            File belongs to group gname (numeric group ID allowed).


find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 | du -b -a | grep 33
This should be the command that finds the password as it searches for a file/directory that has a user named bandit7 and a group named bandit 6, along with the file size looking for specifically 33. 

However, this outputs a large amount of files that is not possible to search through. 
looking through the find manual again we find this option.
    -size n[cwbkMG]
                  File uses less than, more than or exactly n units of space, rounding up.  The following suffixes can be used:
    
                  `b'    for 512-byte blocks (this is the default if no suffix is used)
    
                  `c'    for bytes
    
                  `w'    for two-byte words
    
                  `k'    for kibibytes (KiB, units of 1024 bytes)
    
                  `M'    for mebibytes (MiB, units of 1024 * 1024 = 1048576 bytes)
    
                  `G'    for gibibytes (GiB, units of 1024 * 1024 * 1024 = 1073741824 bytes)
We can use this instead of the du command to refine our search even further.
    bandit6@bandit:/$ find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 
    find: ‘/snap’: Permission denied
    find: ‘/lost+found’: Permission denied
    find: ‘/manpage/manpage3-pw’: Permission denied
    find: ‘/drifter/drifter14_src/axTLS’: Permission denied
    find: ‘/run/pam_pidns’: Permission denied
    find: ‘/run/udisks2’: Permission denied
    find: ‘/run/chrony’: Permission denied
    find: ‘/run/user/12006’: Permission denied
This gives us the least amount of files to look through, a majority of them are permission denied files which means they are being outputted through the standard error stream.
We can direct those files to the /dev/null folder which will get rid of them for us. To do this we add 2> at the end.

    bandit6@bandit:/$ find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 2> /dev/null
    /var/lib/dpkg/info/bandit7.password
    bandit6@bandit:/$ cat /var/lib/dpkg/info/bandit7.password
    Bmnnvf82KzQlfxgAI2d1zYbr1u9pr3E3
We find our password with that command and we use the cat command along with the full path to output the password.

The password is Bmnnvf82KzQlfxgAI2d1zYbr1u9pr3E3



